    "type": "single_choice",
    "text": "A team's Scrum Master notices that during team discussions, one particular idea always seems to 'win' regardless of its actual merit, simply because it was proposed first. What cognitive bias does this MOST likely reflect, and how should it be addressed?",
    "options": {
      "A": "This reflects confirmation bias; the solution is to always select the last idea proposed instead",
      "B": "This reflects anchoring bias; the Scrum Master should introduce techniques like brainstorming all ideas before evaluating any of them, to reduce the influence of the first idea presented",
      "C": "This reflects availability bias; the solution is to eliminate all group discussion entirely",
      "D": "This reflects no particular bias and should be left unaddressed since the first idea is usually the best one"
    },
    "answer": "B",
    "justification": "Correct. This pattern reflects anchoring bias, where the first idea presented disproportionately influences subsequent judgment; brainstorming all ideas before evaluation is a standard technique to reduce this bias's influence.",
    "id": 1030,
    "examen": 4
